```java
class RobotConfig implements Cloneable {
    private String name;
    private List<String> tags;

    @Override
    public RobotConfig clone() {
        RobotConfig copy = new RobotConfig();
        copy.name = this.name;                     // immutable value: safe to share
        copy.tags = new ArrayList<>(this.tags);     // new list: deep-copied
        return copy;
    }
}
```

The key line is `copy.tags = new ArrayList<>(this.tags)` — it builds a brand-new `List` containing the same strings, rather than reusing `this.tags` directly. `name` is a `String`, which is immutable in Java, so sharing the reference is safe: nothing can mutate it out from under either object.

Takeaway: deep-copy every mutable reference field explicitly; immutable fields such as strings, numbers, and enums are safe to copy by reference.
